@startuml
title CoroScope / JoinableScope — child lifetime and joining
<style>
document {
  BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  BoxPadding 10
  ParticipantPadding 10
}
title {
  FontSize 16
  FontColor #111111
}
</style>

actor "Scope owner" as Owner
participant "JoinableScope" as Scope
participant "Child AsyncTask" as Child
participant "JointerTask" as Joiner
participant "BlockingExecutor" as BlockingExecutor
participant "Executor" as Executor

Owner -> Scope : launch(child task)
Scope -> Scope : register child in scope lifetime protocol
Scope -> Child : create child task with scope context
Scope -> Child : start according to launch mode

... child task runs ...

Owner -> Scope : joinAll() or scope destruction
Scope -> Joiner : create / await join task
alt Children remain active
  Scope -> BlockingExecutor : wait for join completion
  note over Scope,Joiner
    Child count and scope death state coordinate
    who completes the join continuation.
  end note
  Child -> Scope : decrement child count at completion
  alt This is the last child and join was requested
    Scope -> Executor : schedule join continuation
    alt Scheduling succeeds
      Executor -> Joiner : resume join task
    else Scheduling fails
      Scope -> Scope : terminate()
    end
  end
  Joiner -> BlockingExecutor : signal synchronous waiter
  BlockingExecutor --> Owner : joinAll() returns
else No children remain
  Scope -> Joiner : complete without waiting
  Joiner --> Owner : joinAll() returns
end

note over Owner,Scope
  JoinableScope::launch wraps launched work in BlockingTask.
  joinAll() and destruction wait for the scope's children;
  this is structured lifetime management, not forced cancellation.
end note
@enduml
